---
import type { Project } from '../data/projects';
interface Props {
	project: Project;
}
const { project } = Astro.props;
---

<a
	id={`project-${project.slug}`}
	href={`/projects/${project.slug}`}
	class:list={[
		'project-card group flex flex-col gap-4 scroll-mt-24 rounded-[1.5px]',
		/* Interior padding for the hover panel, cancelled by a matching negative
		   margin so the CONTENT stays exactly where it was on the grid — the
		   leftmost card's year and title still line up with the nav and footer.
		   The panel itself is what needed the room: without it the tint would
		   stop on the year's cap-height and flush against the image's edges.
		   Sized against the gutters it now grows into. Each card reaches 0.75rem
		   past its column on both sides, and the column gap is gap-x-6 (1.5rem),
		   so two neighbouring panels meet exactly and never overlap; the row gap
		   (gap-y-10, 2.5rem) has a full rem to spare. If either gutter is
		   tightened again, this padding has to come down with it. */
		'p-3 -m-3',
		/* THE HOVER ZOOM IS GONE (2026-08-02, Hiro: don't make the card grow).
		   What used to sit here was `hover:scale-[1.02] focus-within:scale-[1.02]`
		   plus an `origin-top` that existed only to serve it — scaling about the
		   default centre lifted the card's top edge by half the growth, so the
		   heading rose while the gap below it opened, two conflicting reads of one
		   gesture. With no scale there is no origin to argue about.

		   The press scale STAYS. It is a shrink, not a growth, and it is the only
		   thing on the card that answers a pointer-down — the ask was about the
		   card swelling under the cursor, which this is the opposite of. */
		'origin-top active:scale-[0.99]',
		'motion-reduce:active:scale-100',
		/* The lift (translate) and its transition both live in global.css now, with
		   the travelling panel — the panel has to rise by exactly the same
		   distance or it lags behind the card standing on it, so the value is a
		   custom property the script can read rather than a number in a class
		   name. See the .project-card block there, which also records why the
		   long-running argument about how far to lift is finally over. */
		'focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-[var(--focus-ring)]',
	]}
>
	{/* Metadata sits on the page background — reading as a label above the image,
	    not inside a card. */}
	{/* The column has exactly two children, so this gap is the whole
	    title/metadata rhythm — no per-child margins fighting it. */}
	<div class="flex min-w-0 flex-col gap-1">
		{/* Dateline above the title, mirroring the project page's header — the two
		    now open the same way, so arriving on a detail page reads as the card
		    you clicked, enlarged.

		    This REVERSES the earlier call, and the old reasoning is worth keeping
		    because it was sound at the time: the year used to lead the role line
		    instead of taking a line of its own, since spending a whole line of
		    rhythm on four characters pushed the title down the block. What changed
		    is that the role line went (2026-08-02, Hiro) — with nothing left for
		    the year to share, its choice was its own line or nothing.

		    Not an eyebrow label: a bare numeral, no uppercase and no letterspacing,
		    so it reads as a date rather than as a kicker announcing the heading.

		    Body ink, NOT --ink-num (2026-08-02, Hiro — the day after the numerals
		    took the syntax orange). The accent was cut to lift the year out of the
		    muted line without promoting the whole line; on a card whose title had
		    just taken the blue, that put two accent colours in a four-line block and
		    the orange won the entrance. Plain ink keeps the year quiet and lets the
		    title lead. --ink-num is still live on the project pages' datelines, so
		    the token is not orphaned — but the two headers no longer match here, and
		    that was the argument for the year taking its own line at all. */}
		<p class="text-[0.85rem] leading-snug tabular-nums text-[var(--ink)]">{project.year}</p>
		{/* The underline is back (2026-08-02, Hiro), so the <span> is too: it exists
		    to stop the line at the words, keeping the decorative arrow outside it.
		    Targets .project-card__title in global.css. */}
		{/* Stepped down a size when the list went two-up (2026-08-01): at half the
		    old column width, 1.3rem titles ran to three lines on the longer ones
		    and the card read as a headline with a picture under it rather than a
		    piece of work with a name. */}
		{/* 600, down from 700 (2026-08-02, Hiro). This was tried twice before and
		    reverted both times (500 and 600, 07-24) — but under Supreme, and the
		    face has changed twice since. Archivo's 700 is a heavier cut than
		    Supreme's was, and it's now carrying --ink-title's blue as well, so the
		    weight and the colour were both shouting. 600 is a real file in the
		    loaded range (400;500;600;700 in Layout.astro), not a rounding — see
		    the .btn note in global.css for what happens when it isn't.
		    500 is the next step down and also real, if this still reads heavy. */}
		<h3 class="text-base font-semibold tracking-tight text-[var(--ink-title)] md:text-[1.1rem] md:leading-tight">
			<span class="project-card__title">{project.title}</span>
			{/* A right-pointing arrow that appears on hover with the underline,
			    neither of them animated. It has been through three states in one
			    day (2026-08-02) — wiping in and tilting to ↗, then drawn at rest on
			    every card, now hidden until hover — so read the .project-card__arrow
			    note in global.css before changing it rather than inferring intent
			    from whichever state you happened to find. Decorative; the whole
			    card is the link. */}
			<svg
				viewBox="0 0 24 24"
				fill="none"
				stroke="currentColor"
				stroke-width="2"
				stroke-linecap="round"
				stroke-linejoin="round"
				aria-hidden="true"
				class="project-card__arrow ml-2 inline-block h-[0.95rem] w-[0.95rem] align-[-0.125em]"
			>
				<line x1="4" y1="12" x2="19" y2="12"></line>
				<polyline points="12 5 19 12 12 19"></polyline>
			</svg>
		</h3>
	</div>

	{/* The image is the card: it carries the border. The whole link zooms and
	    lifts as one unit on hover (scale + translate on the <a>), so the
	    blended metadata above rises along with it. This frame is the only
	    bordered surface, so it's the only thing that can carry a shadow — the
	    lift on the <a> is what keeps the metadata from being left behind. The
	    shadow is hover-only and lives with the other card hover choreography
	    in global.css (.project-card__frame).

	    bg-clip-padding keeps the --surface-sunken bed out of the border area.
	    --line is translucent, so it takes the colour of whatever sits behind
	    it: painting the bed under the border (the default border-box clip)
	    composited it over --surface-sunken instead of the page's paper and rendered
	    the edge ~7/255 darker than the about photo's identical border in
	    light, and ~13/255 lighter in dark. Clipping to the padding box lets
	    it composite over paper, like the about <img> which has no background
	    of its own — same token, same result. Keep the two in step. */}
	<div
		class:list={[
			'project-card__frame',
			'relative aspect-[16/9] w-full overflow-hidden rounded-[1.5px] border border-[var(--line)] bg-[var(--surface-sunken)] bg-clip-padding',
		]}
	>
		<img
			src={project.cover}
			alt={project.title}
			loading="lazy"
			decoding="async"
			class="absolute inset-0 h-full w-full object-cover object-top"
		/>
	</div>
</a>
